    "id": "geography.a.development.causes",
    "topicId": "geography.a.development",
    "title": "Why do development patterns differ?",
    "refs": [
      "5.2b"
    ],
    "minutes": 30,
    "cards": [
      [
        "Physical conditions create opportunities and costs",
        "Access to sea routes, terrain, climate, water and resources can affect farming, transport and exposure to hazards. Landlocked trade often depends on neighbouring countries’ routes and border arrangements. Infrastructure and cooperation can reduce these constraints: landlocked does not mean inevitably poor. Resources do not guarantee widespread benefit either. Explain a pathway and the conditions that could change it, rather than assigning development to latitude or one natural feature."
      ],
      [
        "History leaves changing legacies",
        "Past colonisation, extraction, trade, conflict and investment can shape institutions, infrastructure and who controls land or wealth. Some transport networks were built around exporting resources rather than connecting local services. These are possible historical pathways, not identical histories for every country. Within the UK, industrial growth and later deindustrialisation affected employment and skills differently across places. Present policies and investment can reinforce or change inherited patterns."
      ],
      [
        "Economic advantages can reinforce each other",
        "Markets, skilled workers, suppliers, finance and transport can attract firms, which can create further jobs and investment. This cumulative process can concentrate opportunity, while places losing industry may face adjustment costs. London’s concentration of high-productivity services illustrates why industry mix and connections matter within the UK. Yet output per hour is not household income, and not all residents share the gains. Migration, public investment, technology and policy can alter these patterns."
      ]
    ],
